<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beer Hall Putsch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1923">1923</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Munich Putsch, Hitler Putsch</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Beer Hall Putsch was a failed coup attempt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a> and General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff">Erich Ludendorff</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> on 8 and 9 November 1923. Launched in the Bürgerbräukeller beer hall, it was crushed by the Bavarian police at the Feldherrnhalle, where 16 putschists and four policemen died. Hitler was tried for treason and wrote Mein Kampf in prison.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch.jpg" alt="Beer Hall Putsch" width="320" height="189" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_119-1486,_Hitler-Putsch,_M%C3%BCnchen,_Marienplatz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> declined as a European power. Hitler, who had fought in the German Army but still held Austrian citizenship, shared the common belief that the country had been stabbed in the back by its own government. He remained in the army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and was sent to infiltrate the small German Workers' Party, which he joined on 12 September 1919 and soon led. He also took the political lead of the Kampfbund, a group of Bavarian patriotic associations, with about 15,000 members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sturmabteilung">Sturmabteilung</a> (SA). The coup was inspired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>'s March on Rome.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Hitler came to realise that Gustav Ritter von Kahr, the Minister-President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, sought to control him and was not ready to act against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. On the evening of 8 November 1923, 603 SA men surrounded the Bürgerbräukeller, where Kahr was speaking to 3,000 people, and a machine gun was set up in the auditorium. Hitler fired a shot into the ceiling, jumped on a chair and shouted that the national revolution had broken out. He declared the Bavarian government deposed and announced a new government with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erich-ludendorff">Erich Ludendorff</a>.</p><p>The state police seized the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telegraph">telegraph</a> office and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> exchange and warned Major-General Jakob von Danner, the army's commandant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, who alerted all units. Around 23:00 Danner and other generals compelled Otto von Lossow to repudiate the putsch. The next day Archbishop Michael von Faulhaber and Rupprecht persuaded Kahr to do the same, and a march on the Feldherrnhalle by about 2,000 Nazis met a police cordon.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Hitler escaped immediate arrest but was arrested two days later, and he, Ludendorff, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-rohm">Ernst Röhm</a> and others were charged with high treason. The Nazi newspaper, the Völkischer Beobachter, was banned. The trial lasted 24 days and was widely publicised, giving Hitler a platform. He was sentenced to five years in Landsberg Prison, where he dictated Mein Kampf to Emil Maurice and Rudolf Hess, and was released on 20 December 1924 after about nine months. Afterwards he sought power by legal means. The Nazi dead were later treated as the party's first blood martyrs, and their bloodstained flag became the Blutfahne.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beer Hall Putsch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The National Socialist German Workers’ Party (NSDAP), led by Adolf Hitler since 1921, had failed in the <b>Beer Hall Putsch</b> of 1923 but became the largest party in the Reichstag in July 1932, winning 37 per cent of the vote amid the mass unemployment of the Great Depression.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Adolf Hitler began to model himself and the Nazi Party on Mussolini within a month, and in November 1923 the Nazis attempted a “March on Berlin” that ended in the failed <b>Beer Hall Putsch</b> in Munich.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/fascism">Fascism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>Benito Mussolini’s March on Rome in 1922 won Hitler’s admiration, and the Nazis’ “March on Berlin” of 1923, modelled on it, ended in the failed <b>Beer Hall Putsch</b> in Munich.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nazism">Nazism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Inter-war Bavaria became a conservative stronghold and refuge of the extreme right, where Hitler's <b>Beer Hall Putsch</b> failed in 1923.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-bavaria">Free State of Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
